text One example of a plugin would be a PDF viewer that is instantiated in a when the user navigates to a PDF file. This would count as a plugin regardless of whether the party that implemented the PDF viewer component was the same as that which implemented the user agent itself. However, a PDF viewer application that launches separate from the user agent (as opposed to using the same interface) is not a plugin by this definition. @17,10927,694,102
link navigable document-sequences.html#navigable @17,11041,694,20
text This specification does not define a mechanism for interacting with plugins, as it is expected to be user-agent- and platform-specific. Some UAs might opt to support a plugin mechanism such as the Netscape Plugin API; others might use remote content converters or have built-in support for certain types. Indeed, this specification doesn't require user agents to support plugins at all. success @17,11073,694,81
link [NPAPI] references.html#refsNPAPI @17,11166,694,20
text Browsers should take extreme care when interacting with external content intended for . When third-party software is run with the same privileges as the user agent itself, vulnerabilities in the third-party software become as dangerous as those in the user agent. danger @17,11198,694,61
