h3 2.1.8 Conformance classes @17,11868,694,19
text This specification describes the conformance criteria for user agents (relevant to implementers) and documents (relevant to authors and authoring tool implementers). @17,11899,694,40
text are those that comply with all the conformance criteria for documents. For readability, some of these conformance requirements are phrased as conformance requirements on authors; such requirements are implicitly requirements on documents: by definition, all documents are assumed to have had an author. (In some cases, that author may itself be a user agent — such user agents are subject to additional rules, as explained below.) @17,11951,694,102
text Conforming documents @17,12065,694,20
text For example, if a requirement states that "authors must not use the foobar element", it would imply that documents are not allowed to contain elements named foobar. @17,12097,694,41
text There is no implied relationship between document conformance requirements and implementation conformance requirements. User agents are not free to handle non-conformant documents as they please; the processing model described in this specification applies to implementations regardless of the conformity of the input documents. success @17,12150,694,81
text User agents fall into several (overlapping) categories with different conformance requirements. @17,12243,694,20
text Web browsers and other interactive user agents @17,12275,694,20
text Web browsers that support must process elements and attributes from the found in XML documents as described in this specification, so that users can interact with them, unless the semantics of those elements have been overridden by other specifications. @17,12307,694,61
row @17,12380,694,21
  link "the XML syntax" xhtml.html#the-xhtml-syntax @17,12380,103,21
  link "HTML namespace" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#html-namespace @132,12380,121,21
text A conforming web browser would, upon finding a element in an XML document, execute the script contained in that element. However, if the element is found within a transformation expressed in XSLT (assuming the user agent also supports XSLT), then the processor would instead treat the element as an opaque element that forms part of the transform. @17,12413,694,81
link script scripting.html#the-script-element @17,12506,694,20
link script scripting.html#the-script-element @17,12538,694,20
text Web browsers that support must process documents labeled with an as described in this specification, so that users can interact with them. @17,12570,694,41
row @17,12623,694,20
  link "the HTML syntax" syntax.html#syntax @17,12623,113,20
  link "HTML MIME type" https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/#html-mime-type @142,12623,115,20
text User agents that support scripting must also be conforming implementations of the IDL fragments in this specification, as described in . @17,12655,694,41
text Web IDL @17,12708,694,20
link [WEBIDL] references.html#refsWEBIDL @17,12740,694,20
text Unless explicitly stated, specifications that override the semantics of HTML elements do not override the requirements on DOM objects representing those elements. For example, the element in the example above would still implement the interface. success @17,12772,694,61
row @17,12845,694,21
  link script scripting.html#the-script-element @17,12845,38,21
  link HTMLScriptElement scripting.html#htmlscriptelement @67,12845,133,21
text Non-interactive presentation user agents @17,12878,694,20
text User agents that process HTML and XML documents purely to render non-interactive versions of them must comply to the same conformance criteria as web browsers, except that they are exempt from requirements regarding user interaction. @17,12910,694,61
text Typical examples of non-interactive presentation user agents are printers (static UAs) and overhead displays (dynamic UAs). It is expected that most static non-interactive presentation user agents will also opt to . success @17,12983,694,61
link "lack scripting support" #non-scripted @17,13056,694,20
text A non-interactive but dynamic presentation UA would still execute scripts, allowing forms to be dynamically submitted, and so forth. However, since the concept of "focus" is irrelevant when the user cannot interact with the document, the UA would not need to support any of the focus-related DOM APIs. @17,13088,694,81
text Visual user agents that support the suggested default rendering @17,13181,694,21
text User agents, whether interactive or not, may be designated (possibly as a user option) as supporting the suggested default rendering defined by this specification. @17,13214,694,40
text This is not required. In particular, even user agents that do implement the suggested default rendering are encouraged to offer settings that override this default to improve the experience for the user, e.g. changing the color contrast, using different focus styles, or otherwise making the experience more accessible and usable to the user. @17,13266,694,81
text User agents that are designated as supporting the suggested default rendering must, while so designated, implement the rules defines as the behavior that user agents are to implement. @17,13359,694,41
link "the Rendering section" rendering.html#rendering @17,13412,694,20
text expected @17,13444,694,21
text User agents with no scripting support @17,13477,694,20
text Implementations that do not support scripting (or which have their scripting features disabled entirely) are exempt from supporting the events and DOM interfaces mentioned in this specification. For the parts of this specification that are defined in terms of an events model or in terms of the DOM, such user agents must still act as if events and the DOM were supported. @17,13509,694,81
text Scripting can form an integral part of an application. Web browsers that do not support scripting, or that have scripting disabled, might be unable to fully convey the author's intent. success @17,13602,694,41
text Conformance checkers @17,13655,694,20
text Conformance checkers must verify that a document conforms to the applicable conformance criteria described in this specification. Automated conformance checkers are exempt from detecting errors that require interpretation of the author's intent (for example, while a document is non-conforming if the content of a element is not a quote, conformance checkers running without the input of human judgement do not have to check that elements only contain quoted material). @17,13687,694,101
link blockquote grouping-content.html#the-blockquote-element @17,13800,694,21
link blockquote grouping-content.html#the-blockquote-element @17,13833,694,20
text Conformance checkers must check that the input document conforms when parsed without a  (meaning that no scripts are run, and that the parser's  is ), and should also check that the input document conforms when parsed with a  in which scripts execute, and that the scripts never cause non-conforming states to occur other than transiently during script execution itself. (This is only a "SHOULD" and not a "MUST" requirement because it has been proven to be impossible. ) @17,13865,694,102
link "browsing context" document-sequences.html#concept-document-bc @17,13979,694,20
link "scripting mode" parsing.html#scripting-mode @17,14011,694,20
link Disabled parsing.html#scripting-mode-disabled @17,14043,694,20
link "browsing context" document-sequences.html#concept-document-bc @17,14075,694,21
link [COMPUTABLE] references.html#refsCOMPUTABLE @17,14108,694,20
text The term "HTML validator" can be used to refer to a conformance checker that itself conforms to the applicable requirements of this specification. @17,14140,694,41
text XML DTDs cannot express all the conformance requirements of this specification. Therefore, a validating XML processor and a DTD cannot constitute a conformance checker. Also, since neither of the two authoring formats defined in this specification are applications of SGML, a validating SGML system cannot constitute a conformance checker either. success @17,14193,694,81
text To put it another way, there are three types of conformance criteria: success @17,14286,694,20
text Criteria that can be expressed in a DTD. success @17,14318,694,20
text Criteria that cannot be expressed by a DTD, but can still be checked by a machine. success @17,14350,694,21
text Criteria that can only be checked by a human. success @17,14383,694,20
text A conformance checker must check for the first two. A simple DTD-based validator only checks for the first class of errors and is therefore not a conforming conformance checker according to this specification. success @17,14415,694,61
text Data mining tools @17,14488,694,20
text Applications and tools that process HTML and XML documents for reasons other than to either render the documents or check them for conformance should act in accordance with the semantics of the documents that they process. @17,14520,694,61
text A tool that generates but increases the nesting level for each paragraph and does not increase the nesting level for would not be conforming. @17,14593,694,41
link "document outlines" sections.html#outline @17,14646,694,20
link headings sections.html#concept-heading @17,14678,694,20
text Authoring tools and markup generators @17,14710,694,21
text Authoring tools and markup generators must generate . Conformance criteria that apply to authors also apply to authoring tools, where appropriate. @17,14743,694,40
link "conforming documents" #conforming-documents @17,14795,694,21
text Authoring tools are exempt from the strict requirements of using elements only for their specified purpose, but only to the extent that authoring tools are not yet able to determine author intent. However, authoring tools must not automatically misuse elements or encourage their users to do so. @17,14828,694,60
text For example, it is not conforming to use an element for arbitrary contact information; that element can only be used for marking up contact information for its nearest or element ancestor. However, since an authoring tool is likely unable to determine the difference, an authoring tool is exempt from that requirement. This does not mean, though, that authoring tools can use elements for any block of italics text (for instance); it just means that the authoring tool doesn't have to verify that when the user uses a tool for inserting contact information for an element, that the user really is doing that and not inserting something else instead. @17,14900,694,143
link address sections.html#the-address-element @17,15055,694,20
link article sections.html#the-article-element @17,15087,694,20
link body sections.html#the-body-element @17,15119,694,20
link address sections.html#the-address-element @17,15151,694,21
link article sections.html#the-article-element @17,15184,694,20
text In terms of conformance checking, an editor has to output documents that conform to the same extent that a conformance checker will verify. success @17,15216,694,41
text When an authoring tool is used to edit a non-conforming document, it may preserve the conformance errors in sections of the document that were not edited during the editing session (i.e., an editing tool is allowed to roundtrip erroneous content). However, an authoring tool must not claim that the output is conformant if errors have been so preserved. @17,15269,694,81
text Authoring tools are expected to come in two broad varieties: tools that work from structure or semantic data, and tools that work on a What-You-See-Is-What-You-Get media-specific editing basis (WYSIWYG). @17,15362,694,61
text The former is the preferred mechanism for tools that author HTML, since the structure in the source information can be used to make informed choices regarding which HTML elements and attributes are most appropriate. @17,15435,694,61
text However, WYSIWYG tools are legitimate. WYSIWYG tools should use elements they know are appropriate, and should not use elements that they do not know to be appropriate. This might in certain extreme cases mean limiting the use of flow elements to just a few elements, like , , , and and making liberal use of the attribute. @17,15508,694,81
link div grouping-content.html#the-div-element @17,15601,694,20
link b text-level-semantics.html#the-b-element @17,15633,694,20
link i text-level-semantics.html#the-i-element @17,15665,694,21
link span text-level-semantics.html#the-span-element @17,15698,694,20
link style dom.html#attr-style @17,15730,694,20
text All authoring tools, whether WYSIWYG or not, should make a best effort attempt at enabling users to create well-structured, semantically rich, media-independent content. @17,15762,694,41
text For compatibility with existing content and prior specifications, this specification describes two authoring formats: one based on , and one using a inspired by SGML (referred to as ). Implementations must support at least one of these two formats, although supporting both is encouraged. @17,15815,694,61
link XML xhtml.html#the-xhtml-syntax @17,15888,694,20
link "custom format" syntax.html#writing @17,15920,694,20
link "the HTML syntax" syntax.html#syntax @17,15952,694,21
text Some conformance requirements are phrased as requirements on elements, attributes, methods or objects. Such requirements fall into two categories: those describing content model restrictions, and those describing implementation behavior. Those in the former category are requirements on documents and authoring tools. Those in the second category are requirements on user agents. Similarly, some conformance requirements are phrased as requirements on authors; such requirements are to be interpreted as conformance requirements on the documents that authors produce. (In other words, this specification does not distinguish between conformance criteria on authors and conformance criteria on documents.) @17,15985,694,162
